<% content_for :title, "My Spirituality | Bipolar Sober" %>

<style>
  .my-spirituality-page .ms-opening-quote {
    margin: 0 0 35px;
    padding: 25px;
    border: 3px solid currentColor;
  }

  .my-spirituality-page .ms-opening-quote p {
    margin: 0 0 18px;
    font-size: clamp(23px, 3vw, 38px);
    font-weight: 800;
    line-height: 1.3;
    letter-spacing: -0.025em;
  }

  .my-spirituality-page .ms-opening-quote cite {
    font-size: 13px;
    font-style: normal;
    font-weight: 800;
    letter-spacing: 0.05em;
  }

  .my-spirituality-page .ms-table-wrap {
    width: 100%;
    margin: 30px 0;
    overflow-x: auto;
  }

  .my-spirituality-page .ms-table {
    width: 100%;
    min-width: 460px;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    color: inherit;
    font: inherit;
  }

  .my-spirituality-page .ms-table caption {
    margin-bottom: 15px;
    text-align: left;
    font-size: 13px;
    font-weight: 900;
    letter-spacing: 0.05em;
  }

  .my-spirituality-page .ms-table th,
  .my-spirituality-page .ms-table td {
    padding: 18px;
    border: 2px solid currentColor;
    text-align: left;
    vertical-align: top;
    font-size: 16px;
    line-height: 1.65;
  }

  .my-spirituality-page .ms-table th {
    font-weight: 900;
  }

  .my-spirituality-page .ms-table tbody th {
    width: 28%;
  }
</style>


<main class="my-spirituality-page">

  <!-- HERO -->
  <section class="my-spirituality-hero">
    <div class="my-spirituality-hero-content">

      <p class="my-spirituality-eyebrow">
        BIPOLAR SOBER / SPIRITUALITY / MY SPIRITUALITY
      </p>

      <h1>
        <span>MANY</span>
        <span>PATHS.</span>
        <span>MY OWN</span>
        <span>WAY.</span>
      </h1>

      <p class="my-spirituality-intro">
        BUDDHISM. HINDUISM. TAOISM.<br>
        SHINTO. PAGANISM.<br>
        AND ROOM FOR WHAT I DON'T KNOW.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <section class="my-spirituality-content">

    <!-- 01 / WHAT I BELIEVE -->
    <article class="ms-section ms-section-black">

      <p class="ms-number">01 / WHAT I BELIEVE</p>

      <h2>
        I DON'T WALK<br>
        ONLY<br>
        ONE PATH.
      </h2>

      <div class="ms-copy">

        <blockquote class="ms-opening-quote">
          <p>
            “The lamps are different, but the light is the same.”
          </p>
          <cite>— Rumi</cite>
        </blockquote>

        <p>
          I don't follow one religion exclusively. My spirituality
          draws from Buddhism, Hinduism, Taoism, Shinto, and Paganism:
          their teachings, practices, philosophies, and the questions
          they encourage me to ask.
        </p>

        <p>
          I believe that all religions are correct. By that, I mean
          that I believe spiritual truth exists throughout religious
          traditions, rather than belonging exclusively to one.
          I don't think one religion possesses the whole picture
          while everyone else is simply mistaken.
        </p>

        <p>
          I recognize that traditions make different, sometimes
          contradictory claims. I don't have a perfect explanation
          for how those claims fit together. My belief leaves room
          for the possibility that spiritual reality exceeds
          what any one explanation can express.
        </p>

        <div class="ms-callout">
          I CAN BELIEVE THERE IS TRUTH<br>
          WITHOUT CLAIMING I UNDERSTAND<br>
          THE WHOLE FUCKING THING.
        </div>

      </div>
    </article>


    <!-- 02 / DIFFERENCE AND UNCERTAINTY -->
    <article class="ms-section ms-section-pink">

      <p class="ms-number">02 / DIFFERENT WAYS OF UNDERSTANDING</p>

      <h2>
        I CAN LEARN<br>
        WHILE KEEPING<br>
        QUESTIONS OPEN.
      </h2>

      <div class="ms-copy">

        <p>
          Some traditions recognize one God, others many deities,
          and others approach ultimate reality without centering
          a creator god. I can take those differences seriously
          while finding meaning in more than one approach.
        </p>

        <p>
          Each tradition develops through particular histories,
          cultures, languages, and communities. I want to understand
          those contexts rather than collect appealing ideas
          and pretend they all mean the same thing.
        </p>

        <p>
          My spirituality is personal. The connections I draw
          describe how I approach these teachings; they do not
          speak for every practitioner or resolve their disagreements.
        </p>

        <div class="ms-stack">
          <span>LEARN WHERE AN IDEA COMES FROM.</span>
          <span>LISTEN TO THE PEOPLE WHO PRACTICE IT.</span>
          <span>ACKNOWLEDGE THE DIFFERENCES.</span>
          <span>LEAVE ROOM TO CHANGE MY UNDERSTANDING.</span>
        </div>

      </div>
    </article>


    <!-- 03 / BUDDHIST FOUNDATION -->
    <article class="ms-section ms-section-yellow">

      <p class="ms-number">03 / MY BUDDHIST FOUNDATION</p>

      <h2>
        START WITH<br>
        WHAT IS<br>
        ACTUALLY HERE.
      </h2>

      <div class="ms-copy">

        <p>
          Buddhism is the foundation of my spirituality.
          The Four Noble Truths, Noble Eightfold Path,
          mindfulness, impermanence, non-self, and compassion
          influence how I understand my life.
        </p>

        <p>
          I experience illness, loss, disappointment, uncertainty,
          and change. I also notice how craving and attachment
          affect my relationship with those experiences.
          I want pleasant feelings to stay, unpleasant ones
          to disappear, and circumstances to follow my expectations.
        </p>

        <p>
          Buddhist practice encourages me to investigate those
          reactions. What am I holding onto? What am I resisting?
          What am I doing that contributes to suffering?
          What could help?
        </p>

        <div class="ms-grid">

          <div class="ms-mini-card">
            <span>UNDERSTANDING</span>
            <h3>LOOK CLOSELY.</h3>
            <p>
              Recognize suffering and examine
              the conditions that keep it going.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="ms-mini-card">
            <span>PRACTICE</span>
            <h3>LIVE THE TEACHING.</h3>
            <p>
              Bring awareness, ethical conduct,
              and compassion into my decisions.
            </p>
          </div>

        </div>

      </div>
    </article>


    <!-- 04 / IMPERMANENCE -->
    <article class="ms-section ms-section-purple">

      <p class="ms-number">04 / IMPERMANENCE &amp; THE MIDDLE WAY</p>

      <h2>
        MY LIFE<br>
        DOESN'T STAY<br>
        IN ONE STATE.
      </h2>

      <div class="ms-copy">

        <p>
          My thoughts, emotions, body, relationships, and circumstances
          change. Recognizing impermanence helps me appreciate
          what is present without expecting to preserve it forever.
        </p>

        <p>
          It also challenges the assumption that a painful experience
          describes my entire future. Change may be slow, and I cannot
          dictate its timing, but my current perspective
          is not the only perspective I will ever have.
        </p>

        <p>
          The Middle Way encourages me to examine extremes:
          chasing pleasure until it creates problems,
          or punishing myself as though suffering proves commitment.
          I want a practice I can live with.
        </p>

        <div class="ms-callout">
          I CAN APPRECIATE WHAT IS HERE.<br>
          I CAN CARE FOR WHAT HURTS.<br>
          I CAN ALLOW THINGS TO CHANGE.
        </div>

      </div>
    </article>


    <!-- 05 / METTA -->
    <article class="ms-section ms-section-black">

      <p class="ms-number">05 / METTA — LOVING-KINDNESS</p>

      <h2>
        GOODWILL<br>
        TAKES<br>
        PRACTICE.
      </h2>

      <div class="ms-copy">

        <p>
          Metta is one of my most important practices.
          I deliberately cultivate goodwill toward myself,
          people I love, people I feel neutral toward,
          people I struggle with, and eventually all beings.
        </p>

        <p>
          I offer wishes for safety, happiness, peace,
          and freedom from suffering. I don't have to manufacture
          a particular emotion before practicing.
          I can begin with the intention.
        </p>

        <div class="ms-stack">
          <span>MAY I BE SAFE.</span>
          <span>MAY YOU BE HAPPY.</span>
          <span>MAY WE BE FREE FROM SUFFERING.</span>
          <span>MAY ALL BEINGS LIVE WITH GREATER EASE.</span>
        </div>

        <p>
          I can wish someone well while maintaining boundaries.
          I can recognize their humanity while acknowledging
          that their behavior caused harm.
          Sometimes distance remains the responsible choice.
        </p>

        <p>
          Metta challenges how I treat myself too.
          I can acknowledge mistakes and make repairs
          without concluding that I deserve endless punishment.
          The care I practice toward others belongs
          in my relationship with myself.
        </p>

      </div>
    </article>


    <!-- 06 / COURAGE -->
    <article class="ms-section ms-section-pink">

      <p class="ms-number">06 / GENTLENESS &amp; BRAVERY</p>

      <h2>
        I CAN FACE<br>
        LIFE WITHOUT<br>
        HARDENING.
      </h2>

      <div class="ms-copy">

        <p>
          Shambhala's language of warriorship speaks to me
          through courage, dignity, gentleness, and basic goodness.
          I appreciate the idea that bravery can involve
          acknowledging fear and remaining open.
        </p>

        <p>
          I recognize how avoidance, aggression, or withdrawal
          can become ways of protecting myself.
          I want to understand those responses and develop
          more freedom in how I meet uncertainty.
        </p>

        <p>
          The genuine heart of sadness describes a tenderness
          I find meaningful: allowing vulnerability
          to deepen my connection with others.
          I can be affected by life and still participate in it.
        </p>

        <div class="ms-thought">
          I CAN FEEL AFRAID.<br>
          I CAN REMAIN GENTLE.<br>
          I CAN STILL ACT WITH COURAGE.
        </div>

      </div>
    </article>


    <!-- 07 / SELF-INQUIRY -->
    <article class="ms-section ms-section-yellow">

      <p class="ms-number">07 / HINDUISM &amp; SELF-INQUIRY</p>

      <h2>
        WHO AM I<br>
        BENEATH<br>
        THE LABELS?
      </h2>

      <div class="ms-copy">

        <p>
          Hindu teachings about Atman and Brahman encourage
          me to examine the nature of identity and reality.
          I am interested in the possibility of a deeper Self
          beyond changing thoughts, emotions, and circumstances.
        </p>

        <p>
          I recognize the difference between this and Buddhist
          non-self. Buddhism challenges my attachment to
          a permanent, independent identity. Hindu traditions
          offer different accounts of the Self and its relationship
          with ultimate reality.
        </p>

        <p>
          I find both inquiries meaningful without claiming
          that their answers are identical.
          I can investigate what I take myself to be
          and remain honest about what I do not understand.
        </p>

        <div class="ms-callout">
          WHAT AM I IDENTIFYING WITH?<br>
          WHAT CHANGES?<br>
          WHAT AM I ASSUMING?
        </div>

      </div>
    </article>


    <!-- 08 / RESPONSIBILITY -->
    <article class="ms-section ms-section-purple">

      <p class="ms-number">08 / DHARMA, KARMA &amp; ACTION</p>

      <h2>
        DO MY PART.<br>
        RELEASE<br>
        MY DEMAND.
      </h2>

      <div class="ms-copy">

        <p>
          Dharma encourages me to consider ethical responsibility
          and what my circumstances ask of me.
          Karma reminds me to examine my actions
          and the consequences they contribute to.
        </p>

        <p>
          Through karma yoga, I explore acting sincerely
          without making my commitment depend entirely
          on receiving a particular result.
        </p>

        <div class="ms-grid">

          <div class="ms-mini-card">
            <span>MY PARTICIPATION</span>
            <h3>THE WORK.</h3>
            <p>
              My preparation, intentions, honesty,
              effort, and willingness to follow through.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="ms-mini-card">
            <span>MY ATTACHMENT</span>
            <h3>THE EXPECTATION.</h3>
            <p>
              My demand that life reward my effort
              with the exact outcome I pictured.
            </p>
          </div>

        </div>

        <p>
          Equanimity helps me consider success and failure
          without making either a complete verdict on my worth.
          I can care about consequences while accepting
          that I cannot determine everything.
        </p>

      </div>
    </article>


    <!-- 09 / TAOISM -->
    <article class="ms-section ms-section-black">

      <p class="ms-number">09 / TAOISM &amp; NON-FORCING</p>

      <h2>
        MAYBE I CAN<br>
        STOP FORCING<br>
        EVERYTHING.
      </h2>

      <div class="ms-copy">

        <p>
          Taoism encourages me to contemplate the Tao,
          the Way through which existence unfolds.
          Wu wei invites me to explore action that responds
          appropriately to circumstances with less forcing.
        </p>

        <p>
          Some situations call for planning and persistence.
          Others require me to recognize a limit,
          change my approach, or stop demanding
          that reality cooperate immediately.
        </p>

        <p>
          I appreciate the image of water:
          adaptable, yielding, persistent, and capable
          of tremendous strength. I want to preserve
          my direction while becoming more flexible
          about how I move.
        </p>

        <div class="ms-callout">
          WHERE IS EFFORT USEFUL?<br>
          WHERE IS RIGIDITY<br>
          MAKING THIS HARDER?
        </div>

        <p>
          Simplicity matters too. I can question whether
          more possessions, achievement, pleasure,
          or control will provide the satisfaction
          I keep expecting.
        </p>

      </div>
    </article>


    <!-- 10 / BALANCE -->
    <article class="ms-section ms-section-pink">

      <p class="ms-number">10 / YIN, YANG &amp; ADJUSTMENT</p>

      <h2>
        BALANCE<br>
        KEEPS<br>
        MOVING.
      </h2>

      <div class="ms-copy">

        <p>
          Yin and yang give me a way to contemplate
          interrelated qualities such as activity and rest,
          movement and stillness, light and darkness.
          Their relationship involves continual change.
        </p>

        <p>
          I can notice which qualities a situation calls for.
          Sometimes I need to act; sometimes I need rest.
          Sometimes speaking helps; sometimes listening
          deserves more of my attention.
        </p>

        <p>
          This connects in my own reflection with Buddhist
          impermanence and Hindu equanimity, while retaining
          its distinct Taoist context.
          I don't expect to reach one preferred state
          and remain there indefinitely.
        </p>

      </div>
    </article>


    <!-- 11 / NATURE -->
    <article class="ms-section ms-section-yellow">

      <p class="ms-number">11 / SHINTO, PAGANISM &amp; NATURE</p>

      <h2>
        THIS WORLD<br>
        DESERVES<br>
        MY REVERENCE.
      </h2>

      <div class="ms-copy">

        <p>
          Shinto and Pagan traditions connect my spiritual
          reflection with the natural world.
          I am drawn to practices involving gratitude,
          sacred places, ancestors, deities, and spirits.
        </p>

        <p>
          I recognize that Shinto kami, Pagan deities,
          and different understandings of spirits belong
          to particular traditions.
          I want to learn their meanings rather than
          treat them as interchangeable.
        </p>

        <p>
          Animistic perspectives interest me because
          they invite relationships with a wider community
          of beings. I believe nature has spiritual significance
          beyond what it provides for human use.
        </p>

        <div class="ms-stack">
          <span>PAY ATTENTION TO WHERE I LIVE.</span>
          <span>RECOGNIZE WHAT SUSTAINS ME.</span>
          <span>APPRECIATE WITHOUT POSSESSING.</span>
          <span>LET REVERENCE BECOME CARE.</span>
        </div>

      </div>
    </article>


    <!-- 12 / ANCESTORS -->
    <article class="ms-section ms-section-purple">

      <p class="ms-number">12 / ANCESTORS &amp; PERSONAL MEANING</p>

      <h2>
        THE PEOPLE<br>
        I STILL<br>
        CARRY.
      </h2>

      <div class="ms-copy">

        <p>
          Remembering my grandparents and other people
          who shaped my life is part of my spirituality.
          I experience that remembrance as a continuing
          connection with those I love.
        </p>


        <div class="ms-thought">
          I COME FROM PEOPLE.<br>
          I CARRY THEIR INFLUENCE.<br>
          I CHOOSE WHAT I PASS ON.
        </div>

      </div>
    </article>


    <!-- 13 / ALTAR -->
    <article class="ms-section ms-section-black">

      <p class="ms-number">13 / MY ALTAR &amp; RITUALS</p>

      <h2>
        A PLACE<br>
        TO FUCKING<br>
        REMEMBER.
      </h2>

      <div class="ms-copy">

        <p>
          My altar gives my spiritual life a physical place.
          Offerings, meaningful objects, and deliberate actions
          help me express gratitude and remember
          what I want to give attention to.
        </p>

        <div class="ms-grid">

          <div class="ms-mini-card">
            <span>01</span>
            <h3>LIGHT</h3>
            <p>
              Candles or another source of light
              can mark a moment of attention and remembrance.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="ms-mini-card">
            <span>02</span>
            <h3>OFFERINGS</h3>
            <p>
              Water, coffee, food, flowers,
              or other appropriate gestures of gratitude.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="ms-mini-card">
            <span>03</span>
            <h3>SOUND</h3>
            <p>
              My singing bowl, music, or silence
              can help make a moment intentional.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="ms-mini-card">
            <span>04</span>
            <h3>OBJECTS</h3>
            <p>
              Stones, crystals, photographs, and objects
              connected with meaningful memories or traditions.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="ms-mini-card">
            <span>05</span>
            <h3>PRACTICE</h3>
            <p>
              Prayer, metta, contemplation,
              and time spent paying attention.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="ms-mini-card">
            <span>06</span>
            <h3>GRATITUDE</h3>
            <p>
              Recognizing what I receive
              and considering what I can give.
            </p>
          </div>

        </div>

        <p>
          Shinto's emphasis on sincerity and Pagan teachings
          about reciprocity influence how I approach this.
          I want my practice to express care
          rather than entitlement to a reward.
        </p>

      </div>
    </article>


    <!-- 14 / RENEWAL -->
    <article class="ms-section ms-section-pink">

      <p class="ms-number">14 / RENEWAL &amp; ACCOUNTABILITY</p>

      <h2>
        A NEW START<br>
        STILL NEEDS<br>
        FOLLOW-THROUGH.
      </h2>

      <div class="ms-copy">

        <p>
          I find renewal meaningful: a chance to return
          with attention after disruption or neglect.
          Ritual can help me acknowledge a transition
          and commit to what comes next.
        </p>

        <p>
          I still need to address what happened.
          An offering cannot pay a bill, repair trust,
          or take responsibility on my behalf.
          My spiritual intentions need an expression
          in my conduct.
        </p>

        <p>
          I want to understand suffering, acknowledge harm,
          and make repairs without turning shame
          into my entire identity.
        </p>

        <div class="ms-callout">
          ACKNOWLEDGE.<br>
          REPAIR.<br>
          PRACTICE DIFFERENTLY.
        </div>

      </div>
    </article>


    <!-- 15 / CONNECTIONS -->
    <article class="ms-section ms-section-yellow">

      <p class="ms-number">15 / WHAT I DRAW FROM EACH PATH</p>

      <h2>
        DIFFERENT<br>
        TEACHINGS.<br>
        A SHARED LIFE.
      </h2>

      <div class="ms-copy">

        <p>
          These are the connections I make in my own practice.
          They describe what I return to within each tradition,
          rather than everything that tradition teaches.
        </p>

        <div class="ms-table-wrap">
          <table class="ms-table">

            <caption>
              THE IDEAS I BRING INTO MY DAILY LIFE
            </caption>

            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th scope="col">PATH</th>
                <th scope="col">WHAT I PRACTICE</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>

            <tbody>

              <tr>
                <th scope="row">BUDDHISM</th>
                <td>
                  Mindfulness, understanding suffering,
                  metta, compassion, and loosening attachment.
                </td>
              </tr>

              <tr>
                <th scope="row">HINDUISM</th>
                <td>
                  Self-inquiry, dharma, responsibility,
                  devotion, and action without attachment to results.
                </td>
              </tr>

              <tr>
                <th scope="row">TAOISM</th>
                <td>
                  Flexibility, simplicity, non-forcing,
                  and responding to changing circumstances.
                </td>
              </tr>

              <tr>
                <th scope="row">SHINTO</th>
                <td>
                  Sincerity, gratitude, renewal,
                  and respect for sacred relationships and places.
                </td>
              </tr>

              <tr>
                <th scope="row">PAGANISM</th>
                <td>
                  Reciprocity, nature-centered ritual,
                  ancestral remembrance, and seasonal awareness.
                </td>
              </tr>

            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <p>
          Buddhist non-self and Hindu Atman remain different
          teachings. The Tao is not simply another name
          for Brahman. I can appreciate several perspectives
          while acknowledging where they diverge.
        </p>

      </div>
    </article>


    <!-- 16 / DISCERNMENT -->
    <article class="ms-section ms-section-purple">

      <p class="ms-number">16 / DISCERNMENT &amp; MYSTERY</p>

      <h2>
        I CAN BELIEVE.<br>
        I CAN ALSO<br>
        QUESTION.
      </h2>

      <div class="ms-copy">

        <p>
          I believe spiritual practice requires discernment.
          My thoughts, feelings, and interpretations
          deserve examination, including when they
          feel especially meaningful.
        </p>

        <p>
          I can appreciate an experience without immediately
          deciding it contains an instruction.
          I can recognize a coincidence, feel moved,
          and leave its explanation open.
        </p>

        <div class="ms-stack">
          <span>WHAT DID I ACTUALLY EXPERIENCE?</span>
          <span>WHAT AM I INFERRING?</span>
          <span>WHAT ELSE MIGHT EXPLAIN IT?</span>
          <span>WHAT DO I NEED TO CHECK?</span>
        </div>

        <p>
          My spirituality belongs alongside treatment,
          practical information, and trusted support.
          I can remain open to spiritual possibilities
          while acknowledging the limits of my judgment.
        </p>

        <div class="ms-callout">
          WONDER CAN LEAVE ROOM<br>
          FOR “I DON'T FUCKING KNOW.”
        </div>

      </div>
    </article>


    <!-- 17 / RECOVERY -->
    <article class="ms-section ms-section-black">

      <p class="ms-number">17 / SPIRITUALITY &amp; RECOVERY</p>

      <h2>
        CONNECTION<br>
        GIVES ME<br>
        PERSPECTIVE.
      </h2>

      <div class="ms-copy">

        <p>
          Recovery gives these teachings a place in
          my actual life. I can ask for help, accept limits,
          examine my intentions, and consider
          what my choices contribute to.
        </p>

        <p>
          A Higher Power, for me, does not require
          adopting someone else's exact understanding of God.
          I can begin by recognizing that my immediate
          thoughts and desires are part of a much larger world.
        </p>

        <p>
          My spirituality supports the work I still have to do:
          staying honest, participating in care,
          honoring commitments, and dealing with consequences.
          It also helps me remember what I want
          to remain present for.
        </p>

      </div>
    </article>


    <!-- 18 / DAILY PRACTICE -->
    <article class="ms-section ms-section-pink">

      <p class="ms-number">18 / WHAT THIS LOOKS LIKE</p>

      <h2>
        BRING IT<br>
        INTO MY<br>
        EVERYDAY LIFE.
      </h2>

      <div class="ms-copy">

        <p>
          Understanding an idea and living it are different
          kinds of work. I can value nonattachment and still cling.
          I can practice metta and still become angry.
          My practice gives me somewhere to return.
        </p>

        <div class="ms-practice-grid">

          <div>
            <span>01</span>
            <p>
              Practice metta toward myself
              and other beings.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div>
            <span>02</span>
            <p>
              Notice nature from wherever
              my body allows me to be.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div>
            <span>03</span>
            <p>
              Tend my altar and make
              meaningful offerings.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div>
            <span>04</span>
            <p>
              Study traditions with attention
              to their differences and histories.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div>
            <span>05</span>
            <p>
              Remember my ancestors
              and examine what I carry forward.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div>
            <span>06</span>
            <p>
              Ask where effort helps
              and where I am forcing an outcome.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div>
            <span>07</span>
            <p>
              Take responsibility and accept
              support when I need it.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div>
            <span>08</span>
            <p>
              Leave room for uncertainty,
              reflection, and changing my mind.
            </p>
          </div>

        </div>

      </div>
    </article>


    <!-- ENDING -->
    <article class="ms-ending">

      <p>
        AWARENESS. COMPASSION.<br>
        RESPONSIBILITY. CONNECTION.
      </p>

      <h2>
        MANY PATHS.<br>
        MY OWN<br>
        WAY.
      </h2>

      <p class="ms-ending-copy">
